Local planning: landing point selection

Even though a rudimentary path planning algorithm outlined above is capable of transporting
the Hopcopter to the goal, a more sophisticated approach that accounts for terrain conditions is
required to enable stable and reliable traversal over uneven, cluttered, or sloped terrain. The
Hopcopter discretizes a set of candidate landing points within a specified radius and computes
a weighted score for each candidate, following an approach similar to the footstep planning
method described by Fankhauser et al. [15]. To adapt the weighted scoring scheme to hopping
robots, the evaluation criteria include:
1. Slope of the surface
2. Distance from nearby obstacles
3. Distance from the takeoff point
4. Proximity to the edges of the surface
Only after full consideration of these criteria, Hopcopter will pick the landing point with the
highest score.
Criteria 1 takes into account the slope of the surface of the landing point. This is important
because it directly affects the orientation of the friction cone. Landing on surfaces that have a
higher slope will limit the allowed landing states and increase the risk of slippage. Hence, higher
slopes will be made to deduct from the score.
Criteria 2 considers the distance of nearby obstacles. Due to Hopcopter’s inverted pendulum
swing, there is a risk of collision with nearby obstacles during the stance phase. To reduce this
